/-!
# A maximal bad quotient has a reduced base

Source: Stacks Project, More on Flatness, Tag 05FS,
`lemma-fibres-irreducible-flat-projective`, first proof paragraph
(blueprint: lem-trf-projectivity-maximal-bad-reduced-quotient).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v w

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace Algebra

/-- A nonprojective flat finite type algebra over a Noetherian ring admits a proper radical
quotient that remains nonprojective, while every further nonzero ideal quotient is projective.
The quotient algebra structures are Mathlib's canonical
`Ideal.Quotient.algebraQuotientMapQuotient`; no algebra structure is chosen.
The fibre `p.asIdeal.Fiber S` is `κ(p) ⊗[R] S`. Its further base change to a field `K`
is canonically `S ⊗[R] K`, by tensor associativity and symmetry.
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Spec.residueFieldIso` identifies the ideal residue field with
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.residueField` of the affine scheme.
The test-field universe contains both ring universes, and permits arbitrary enlargement `w`.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Flatness, Tag 05FS,
`lemma-fibres-irreducible-flat-projective`, first proof paragraph
(blueprint: lem-trf-projectivity-maximal-bad-reduced-quotient).

Proof:
1. For every ideal `H`, flatness descends to `R/H → S/HS`: for an injection of
   `R/H`-modules, use flatness over `R` and the natural tensor-over-quotient
   identifications of lem-weil-pbc-tensor-over-quotient-ring. Images of finitely many
   algebra generators prove finite type (def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra).
2. The set of ideals `H` for which `S/HS` is not projective contains zero.
   Apply lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-ideal-set-maximal to choose a maximal member `I`.
   It is proper because the zero module over the zero ring is projective, by the
   lifting characterization of def-weil-pbc-projective-module. All strictly larger
   ideals have projective quotient.
3. The radical `L` of `I` is finitely generated by Noetherianity. If its generators
   have powers of exponents `n₁, …, nₜ` in `I`, then `L^N ≤ I` for
   `N = 1 + ∑ (nᵢ - 1)`, by the pigeonhole principle on monomials; the empty list
   gives `L = 0` and `N = 1`. Thus `L/I` is nilpotent. If `I ≠ L`, maximality makes
   `S/LS` projective. Step 1 and lem-trf-local-nilpotent-projective-flat-lift then
   make `S/IS` projective, a contradiction. Hence `I` is radical.
4. Put `A = R/I`, `B = S/IS`. Properness gives nontriviality; ideal generators
   descend, giving Noetherianity; radicality gives reducedness. Step 1 gives finite
   type and flatness. A nonzero ideal `J` of `A` corresponds to `H > I` in `R`.
   The canonical isomorphisms `A/J ≃ R/H` and `B/JB ≃ S/HS` transport projectivity
   from step 2, using def-weil-pbc-projective-module. This includes `J = A`.
5. A prime `q` of `A` contracts to `p` in `R`, with the same residue field
   (def-weil-residue-field). For every field extension `K`, tensor-over-quotient
   (lem-weil-pbc-tensor-over-quotient-ring), associativity and symmetry identify
   `B ⊗[A] K` with `S ⊗[R] K`. The latter is a nonzero domain by hypothesis,
   proving the required fibre condition. -/
theorem exists_maximal_bad_reduced_quotient
    (R : Type u) (S : Type v) [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]
    [IsNoetherianRing R]
    (hfinite : RingHom.FiniteType (algebraMap R S))
    (hflat : RingHom.Flat (algebraMap R S))
    (hfibres : ∀ (p : PrimeSpectrum R) (K : Type (max u v w)) [Field K]
      [Algebra p.asIdeal.ResidueField K],
      IsDomain (K ⊗[p.asIdeal.ResidueField] p.asIdeal.Fiber S))
    (hbad : ¬ Module.Projective R S) :
    ∃ I : Ideal R, I ≠ ⊤ ∧ I.IsRadical ∧
      Nontrivial (R ⧸ I) ∧ IsNoetherianRing (R ⧸ I) ∧ IsReduced (R ⧸ I) ∧
      RingHom.FiniteType (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (S ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R S))) ∧
      RingHom.Flat (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (S ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R S))) ∧
      (∀ (q : PrimeSpectrum (R ⧸ I)) (K : Type (max u v w)) [Field K]
        [Algebra q.asIdeal.ResidueField K],
        IsDomain (K ⊗[q.asIdeal.ResidueField]
          q.asIdeal.Fiber (S ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R S)))) ∧
      ¬ Module.Projective (R ⧸ I) (S ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R S)) ∧
      (∀ J : Ideal (R ⧸ I), J ≠ ⊥ →
        Module.Projective ((R ⧸ I) ⧸ J)
          ((S ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R S)) ⧸
            J.map (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (S ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R S))))) := by
  sorry

end Algebra
